    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe `gQ` mapping only restores N-1 cursors of the previous N multiple cursors.\nThe position of the \"current cursor\" is not restored and it remains wherever it was before `gQ` was pressed. Thus, the full multiple cursors state is not faithfully restored.\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- place multiple cursors using `Q`\n- clear multiple cursors with `Ctrl-L`\n- move current cursor away from current position (to make the problem more obvious) \n- press `gQ`\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`gQ` should restore **all** cursor positions including resetting the current cursor to its previous position. The position of the current cursor should also be treated as being one of the multiple cursors -- at least if follow-mode was on.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-1651+gcb76690546\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\ndoes not apply\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nArch Linux\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nKitty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\nAUR",
